arm uses a load store model for memory access 
    this means that only load (LDR) and store (STR) instructions can access memory 

on arm data must be moved to a register before working on it. Unlike x86 this has to be done 

For example if we wish to increment a 32 bit value at a particular adress we would need to first load it into a register, then increment it in the register, and then store it at the particular address 

Offset basically means distance from a starting address
so you need to understand a few words before understanding this term

Base adress - is basically the *starting point*, so where youre starting
Offset - is basically the distance that takes you to get to your absolute destination (final point), or, more broadly speaking, a number that you add to a starting point to get to a new destination
Absolute adress - is basically the adress youll get if you sum base address and offset 

As an example, if you had 
Base adress - 1000 (starting point)
Offset - 4 (distance from absolute address)
Absolute adress = 1000 + 4 = 1004 (the final address)

i find this fun because its a way of using indexing
imagine if you had a list in c: 
    int list_here[3] = {10,20,30};
and its in memory adress 1000 for example and this 1000 is kept at a register x1 
so its indexes would be at
index  value
1000   10
1004   20
1008   30
and you need to find the value of 30 
so what you do is you load the memory address in a register, and then you create an absolute address
    ldr r0, [x1, #8]       @ x1 is the base adress, #8 is the offset. 1000 + 8 = 1008.
and you then get the value of 30 in r0.

