.data  /* this is where data is stored and adress CANT be explicitly predicted. we need literal pool*/
    var1: .word 3  /* variable 1 in memory. This is called a label, its later replaced with 
                      an adress by the assembler */
    var2: .word 4  /* variable 2 in memory */

.text          /* start of the text (code) section  I RECOMEND DOING THIS */ 
.global _start

_start:
    ldr r0, adr_var1  @ load the memory address of var1 via label adr_var1 into R0 
    ldr r1, adr_var2  @ load the memory address of var2 via label adr_var2 into R1 
    ldr r2, [r0]      @ load the value (0x03) at memory address found in R0 to register R2  
    str r2, [r1]      @ store the value found in R2 (0x03) to the memory address found in R1 
    bkpt             

adr_var1: .word var1  /* address to var1 stored here 
                literal meaning: store 32 bits and then fill in this value of var1 into it*/
adr_var2: .word var2  /* address to var2 stored here */

- code section end - 
-
- code explanation start- 

So what ive noticed is that this is extremely similar of how pointers work in C

First off id like to mention that this bottom part of the code is called a *literal pool*, what it basically does is it serves as a way of inputting variables into our code. Because instructions cant hold such big values as an immediate value for example mov r0 , 0x111111 or whatever we have to use a literal
More official term: *a block of memory embedded in the code to hold literal values*

Now lets talk about how its similar to c 
So first off we define 
    adr_var1: .word var1
What this is, is like an interpretation of 
    int *var1_ptr = &var1;
I would like to mention, if you noticed, that we use the memory adress in our arm code instead of the variable in C we just defined. Because we need to load it.

Now lets look at the _start section code 
    ldr r0, adr_var1 
what this did is it basically loaded the *contents* of adr_var1 to r0.
C interpretation 
    r0 = var1_ptr;
so this does the same thing
pointer dereferencing
    ldr r2, [r0]
what this did is it took the value of r0 which is a memory adress and then grabbed its actual value 
C interpretation
    int r2 = *var1_ptr;

and str writes a register’s value to a memory address

What we need to understand from it 
1. Literal pools must be used when were working with data that we cant make immediate 
2. to store data and load it we need to use these literal pools 

-code explanation end -

-- parapraph 1 Immediate value as offset -- 
str Ra, [Rb, imm]
ldr Ra, [Rc, imm]

in this we use an immediate value as an offset 

.data
var1: .word 3
var2: .word 4

.text
.global _start

_start:
    ldr r0, adr_var1  @ load the memory address of var1 via label adr_var1 into R0
    ldr r1, adr_var2  @ load the memory address of var2 via label adr_var2 into R1
    ldr r2, [r0]      @ load the value (0x03) at memory address found in R0 to register R2 
    str r2, [r1, #2]  @ address mode: offset. Store the value found in R2 (0x03) to the memory address found in R1 plus 2. Base register (R1) unmodified. 
    str r2, [r1, #4]! @ address mode: pre-indexed. Store the value found in R2 (0x03) to the memory address found in R1 plus 4. Base register (R1) modified: R1 = R1+4 
    ldr r3, [r1], #4  @ address mode: post-indexed. Load the value at memory address found in R1 to register R3. Base register (R1) modified: R1 = R1+4 
    bkpt

adr_var1: .word var1   @value = 3
adr_var2: .word var2   @value =4 

-tracing-
lets say these are the values 

0x20000: 03 00 00 00   <- var1 = 3
0x20004: 04 00 00 00   <- var2 = 4
pool:    adr_var1 = 0x20000, adr_var2 = 0x20004

#	Instruction	        What happens	                                    State after
1	ldr r0, adr_var1	read the pool cell	                                r0 = 0x20000
2	ldr r1, adr_var2	read the pool cell	                                r1 = 0x20004
3	ldr r2, [r0]	    read memory at 0x20000	                            r2 = 3
4	str r2, [r1, #2]	write 3 to 0x20004+2 = 0x20006	                    r1 still 0x20004
5	str r2, [r1, #4]!	r1 becomes 0x20008 first, then write 3 to 0x20008	r1 = 0x20008
6	ldr r3, [r1], #4	read memory at 0x20008 first, then r1 += 4	r3 = 3, r1 = 0x2000C

